  "prompt_text": "The figure compares a healthy brain with two injured or inflammatory conditions, EAE and SCI, to show how the cellular environment around blood vessels changes. In the healthy brain, blue astrocytes are evenly distributed and blood vessels are relatively isolated, with only occasional macrophage-like cells nearby and no obvious immune-cell buildup or scarring.\n\nIn contrast, both disease panels show a dense, localized lesion centered around vessels. The damaged region contains many green reactivated astrocytes, yellow T cells, blue macrophages, and numerous pink fibroblasts that become larger and more abundant, suggesting activation and tissue remodeling. This creates a crowded, disorganized cellular cluster that replaces the normal sparse architecture.\n\nThe two disease states differ in emphasis. In EAE, the lesion appears strongly inflammatory, with many infiltrating T cells mixed with macrophages, reactive astrocytes, and activated fibroblasts around the vessel. In SCI, the response seems more dominated by a compact fibroblast-rich scar-like core, surrounded by many reactive astrocytes and macrophages, with fewer visible T cells. Overall, the image highlights that disease or injury converts a normal neurovascular environment into an inflammatory, fibrotic lesion.",
